Local bluez patch, not upstream. Applied by scripts/install-btvirt.sh to the
`apt-get source bluez` tree AFTER bluez-4ff7deaf8c-emulator-receiving-side-
handle.patch and bluez-local-emulator-legacy-adv-every-interval.patch, whose
post-image it is cut against, before building emulator/btvirt.

  tag:      max-btdev-24 (the name the provenance sidecar carries)
  subject:  emulator: hold twenty-four controllers instead of sixteen
  cut:      2026-10-13, against bluez 5.82-1.1 (Debian) + 4ff7deaf8c
            + adv-every-interval, 1 hunk
  files:    emulator/btdev.c only

WHAT IT CHANGES

`MAX_BTDEV_ENTRIES` goes from 16 to 24. It sizes `btdev_list`, the one array
of controller pointers, and bounds every loop over it (add_btdev, del_btdev,
valid_btdev, the two address lookups, inquiry_callback's cursor, the
broadcast loops). Nothing else pins sixteen: the slot index becomes a
`uint8_t` address byte, the `-l` count is an unbounded atoi, and connection
handles are allocated per controller.

WHY 24

Sixteen was the only reason periculum's regression/ble_room_20 could not run
here: `btvirt -L -l17` exits at once with "Failed to open Virtual HCI
device". Twenty-four is the twenty-node room plus four of headroom, and it is
the number periculum's `MAX_CONTROLLERS` (eec45df) validates rooms against,
so the two must move together.

THE MEASUREMENT (periculum order 701, 2026-10-13, artefacts /home/lew/ci/p701)

On the patched binary `-L -l20` brings up twenty controllers and `-l25`
fails with the old message, and ble_room_20 ran green three of three, 380 of
380 ordered probes answered each run. Built binary md5
d4a62600d53aa83e102d71d9034c20a7.

patch(1) ignores this header.

